\title{An Abstract Bilinear Pairing and the Algebraic Core of BLS Signatures,
Aggregation, and the MOV Reduction}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 23, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Pairing-based cryptography rests on a single algebraic object: a bilinear map
$e : G \times G \to T$ from an additively written abelian group $G$ (the points
of an elliptic curve) to a multiplicatively written abelian group $T$ (a group
of roots of unity). We isolate the minimal interface this object must satisfy ---
biadditivity in each argument --- and show that the entire protocol layer of the
Boneh--Lynn--Shacham (BLS) signature scheme follows from it by elementary
algebra, with no recourse to the analytic construction of the Weil or Tate
pairing. We prove: (i) the family of bilinearity laws, including the
scalar-sliding identity $e(n\cdot p, q) = e(p,q)^n$ over $\NN$ and $\ZZ$, the
joint law $e(a\cdot p, b\cdot q) = e(p,q)^{ab}$, and the sum-to-product law
$e(\sum_i f_i, q) = \prod_i e(f_i, q)$; (ii) BLS completeness
$e(x\cdot H, g) = e(H, x\cdot g)$ and aggregate completeness
$e(\sum_i x_i\cdot H_i, g) = \prod_i e(H_i, x_i\cdot g)$, the algebraic content
of short aggregate signatures; (iii) the binding property as point separation
under nondegeneracy, equivalent to injectivity of the induced character map;
(iv) the alternating refinement characterizing the genuine Weil pairing, from
which antisymmetry $e(p,q)\cdot e(q,p) = 1$ follows; and (v) the
Menezes--Okamoto--Vanstone (MOV) reduction as a faithful congruence,
transporting the elliptic-curve discrete logarithm problem (ECDLP) into a
discrete logarithm in $T$ and recovering the secret exactly modulo
$\ord(e(g,g))$, with full recovery precisely when this order dominates
$\ord(g)$. Every statement is backed by a formally verified proof.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Cryptographic pairings enable a class of protocols unattainable with classical
discrete-log groups alone: short signatures, signature aggregation,
identity-based encryption, and succinct non-interactive arguments. The canonical
instance is the Weil pairing on the $r$-torsion subgroup $E[r]$ of an elliptic
curve $E$, a deep construction drawing on divisors and function fields. Yet the
protocols that consume a pairing never use anything beyond a small set of
algebraic identities. This paper makes that precise: we axiomatize the pairing by
its characteristic property --- biadditivity into a multiplicative target --- and
derive the full BLS protocol layer, its aggregate variant, the binding property,
the alternating Weil refinement, and the MOV reduction from this interface alone.

The methodological payoff is twofold. First, the proofs become elementary and
modular: each cryptographic guarantee is a one- or two-line consequence of a
named algebraic lemma. Second, the boundary between ``what the construction
gives'' and ``what the protocol needs'' becomes explicit. BLS completeness and
aggregation need only biadditivity; binding needs nondegeneracy; the
Weil-specific antisymmetry needs the alternating axiom; and the MOV reduction
needs only biadditivity plus the order of a single target element.

\subsection{Notation}
Throughout, $G$ is an additively written abelian group (strengthened to a group
where inverses are required) and $T$ is a multiplicatively written abelian
group. We write $n\cdot p$ for the $n$-fold sum of $p\in G$ ($n\in\NN$ or
$\ZZ$), $t^n$ for the $n$-th power in $T$, $1$ for the identity of $T$,
$\ord(t)$ for the order of $t\in T$, and $a\equiv b \pmod m$ for congruence of
naturals. The symbol $e$ always denotes the pairing map.

\section{The pairing interface}

\begin{definition}[Pairing]\label{def:pairing}
A \emph{pairing} from $G$ to $T$ is a map $e : G \times G \to T$ that is
additive-to-multiplicative in each argument:
\[
  e(a + b,\ q) = e(a, q)\cdot e(b, q), \qquad
  e(p,\ a + b) = e(p, a)\cdot e(p, b).
\]
These two axioms are the complete definition; no further structure is assumed.
\end{definition}

The source group models the elliptic curve point group (secret keys act by
scalar multiplication); the target group models the multiplicative group
$\mu_r \subset K^\times$ of $r$-th roots of unity.

\section{Bilinearity: the derived laws}

\begin{lemma}[Unit on the boundary]\label{lem:unit}
$e(0, q) = 1$ and $e(p, 0) = 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $a=b=0$ in additivity on the left: $e(0,q) = e(0,q)\cdot e(0,q)$; in a group
$x = x\cdot x$ forces $x = 1$. The right identity is the mirror argument.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Scalar sliding]\label{lem:nsmul}
For all $n\in\NN$, $e(n\cdot p, q) = e(p,q)^n$ and $e(p, n\cdot q) = e(p,q)^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$. Base $n=0$ is Lemma~\ref{lem:unit} and $t^0 = 1$. Step:
$e((n+1)\cdot p, q) = e(n\cdot p, q)\cdot e(p,q) = e(p,q)^n\cdot e(p,q) =
e(p,q)^{n+1}$, using additivity and the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Joint scalar law]\label{lem:joint}
For all $a,b\in\NN$, $e(a\cdot p, b\cdot q) = e(p,q)^{ab}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:nsmul} in each slot: $e(a\cdot p, b\cdot q) = e(p, b\cdot
q)^a = (e(p,q)^b)^a = e(p,q)^{ab}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sum to product]\label{lem:sum}
For a finite index set $s$ and $f : s \to G$,
$ e\!\big(\sum_{i\in s} f_i,\ q\big) = \prod_{i\in s} e(f_i, q). $
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Finite-set induction. Empty: the empty sum is $0$, the empty product is $1$, and
$e(0,q)=1$. Insertion of a new index $a$ gives $e(f_a + \sum_i f_i, q) = e(f_a,
q)\cdot e(\sum_i f_i, q)$ by additivity, then the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Inverses and $\ZZ$-grading]\label{lem:neg}
When $G$ is a group, $e(-p, q) = e(p,q)^{-1}$, and for all $n\in\ZZ$,
$e(n\cdot p, q) = e(p,q)^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $e(p,q)\cdot e(-p,q) = e(0,q) = 1$ we get $e(-p,q) = e(p,q)^{-1}$. For the
$\ZZ$ law split $n = m$ or $n = -m$ with $m\in\NN$; the nonnegative case is
Lemma~\ref{lem:nsmul}, the negative case combines the inverse law with
$t^{-m} = (t^m)^{-1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{BLS signatures and aggregation}

Public parameters fix a generator $g\in G$. A signer holds secret key
$x\in\NN$ and publishes public key $X = x\cdot g$. To sign a message with
hash-to-curve value $H\in G$, the signer outputs $\sigma = x\cdot H$. A verifier
with $(g, X, H, \sigma)$ accepts iff $e(\sigma, g) = e(H, X)$.

\begin{theorem}[BLS completeness]\label{thm:bls}
For all $g, H\in G$ and $x\in\NN$, $ e(x\cdot H,\ g) = e(H,\ x\cdot g). $
Hence the verification equation holds for every honest signature
$\sigma = x\cdot H$ with $X = x\cdot g$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:nsmul}, $e(x\cdot H, g) = e(H,g)^x = e(H, x\cdot g)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate completeness]\label{thm:agg}
For a finite index set $s$, generator $g$, hash points $H : s\to G$, and secret
keys $x : s\to\NN$,
\[
  e\!\Big(\sum_{i\in s} x_i\cdot H_i,\ g\Big) = \prod_{i\in s} e\big(H_i,\ x_i\cdot g\big).
\]
The aggregate signature $\sigma_{\mathrm{agg}} = \sum_i x_i\cdot H_i$ is a single
group element verifying against the product of per-signer pairings.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:sum} on the left to get $\prod_i e(x_i\cdot H_i, g)$, then
rewrite each factor by Theorem~\ref{thm:bls}.
\end{proof}

This is the algebraic content of \emph{short aggregate signatures}: $n$
signatures compress to one group element while verification fans out into $n$
publicly-checkable factors.

\section{Binding via nondegeneracy}

A pairing is \emph{left-nondegenerate} if $\forall a,\ (\forall q,\ e(a,q)=1)
\Rightarrow a = 0$.

\begin{theorem}[Point separation]\label{thm:sep}
If $e$ is left-nondegenerate and $e(p_1, q) = e(p_2, q)$ for all $q$, then
$p_1 = p_2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For each $q$, $e(p_1 - p_2, q) = e(p_1,q)\cdot e(p_2,q)^{-1} = 1$ by additivity
and Lemma~\ref{lem:neg}. Nondegeneracy on $p_1 - p_2$ gives $p_1 - p_2 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Character homomorphisms]\label{def:char}
For fixed $q$ (resp.\ $p$), the maps $p\mapsto e(p,q)$ and $q\mapsto e(p,q)$ are
additive group homomorphisms $G \to \mathrm{Additive}\,T$, packaging the two
biadditivity axioms in the standard homomorphism API ($e(0,q)=1$ is
$\mathrm{map\_zero}$; additivity is $\mathrm{map\_add}$).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Nondegeneracy is injectivity]\label{thm:nondeg}
Left-nondegeneracy is equivalent to injectivity of the character map
$\chi(p) = (q\mapsto e(p,q))$:
\[
  \big(\forall a,\ (\forall q,\ e(a,q)=1)\Rightarrow a=0\big) \iff \text{$\chi$ injective}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\Rightarrow)$ If $\chi(p_1)=\chi(p_2)$ then $e(p_1,q)=e(p_2,q)$ for all $q$;
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}. $(\Leftarrow)$ If $e(a,q)=1$ for all $q$ then
$\chi(a)=\chi(0)$, so $a=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{The alternating refinement: the genuine Weil pairing}

\begin{definition}[Alternating pairing]\label{def:alt}
An \emph{alternating pairing} is a pairing $e$ on a group $G$ additionally
satisfying $e(p,p)=1$ for all $p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Antisymmetry]\label{thm:antisym}
For an alternating pairing, $ e(p,q)\cdot e(q,p) = 1$, equivalently
$e(q,p) = e(p,q)^{-1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $1 = e(p+q, p+q) = e(p,p)\cdot e(p,q)\cdot e(q,p)\cdot e(q,q)$ by
biadditivity; the alternating axiom kills $e(p,p)=e(q,q)=1$, leaving
$e(p,q)\cdot e(q,p)=1$.
\end{proof}

Antisymmetry distinguishes the Weil pairing from a generic biadditive map and
explains why self-pairing leaks nothing: $e(X,X)=1$ for every public key $X$.

\section{The MOV reduction: a Cryptography $\leftrightarrow$ Algebra bridge}

\begin{theorem}[The MOV map]\label{thm:movmap}
For all $g\in G$ and $x\in\NN$, $ e(x\cdot g,\ g) = e(g, g)^{x}. $
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:nsmul} in the first slot with $p=q=g$.
\end{proof}

Thus the ECDLP instance ``$X = x\cdot g$'' becomes the DLP instance
``$e(X,g) = e(g,g)^x$'' in $T$.

\begin{theorem}[Faithfulness of the reduction]\label{thm:movred}
For all $g\in G$ and $a,b\in\NN$,
\[
  e(a\cdot g,\ g) = e(b\cdot g,\ g) \iff a \equiv b \pmod{\ord\!\big(e(g,g)\big)}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:movmap} the equality is $e(g,g)^a = e(g,g)^b$; equal powers
of $t$ are characterized by congruence of exponents modulo $\ord(t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Full recovery]\label{thm:movrec}
Let $n\in\NN$ with $n \le \ord(e(g,g))$. If $a,b < n$ and
$e(a\cdot g, g) = e(b\cdot g, g)$, then $a = b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:movred} gives $a\equiv b \pmod{\ord(e(g,g))}$; since $a,b < n
\le \ord(e(g,g))$ both equal their own residues, so $a=b$.
\end{proof}

Taking $n = \ord(g)$: whenever the order of $e(g,g)$ is at least the order of $g$
(small embedding degree), the finite-field DLP solver returns the unique secret
in $0 \le x < \ord(g)$. This is the precise reason curves of small embedding
degree are cryptographically broken.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BLS aggregate verification} (Theorem~\ref{thm:agg}). Given $(g,
\{(H_i,X_i)\}, \sigma_{\mathrm{agg}})$, accept iff $e(\sigma_{\mathrm{agg}}, g) =
\prod_i e(H_i, X_i)$. Cost: one left pairing, $n$ right pairings, $n-1$ target
multiplications.
\item \textbf{MOV attack} (Theorems~\ref{thm:movmap}--\ref{thm:movrec}). On a
small-embedding-degree curve, compute $u = e(X, g)$, $h = e(g,g)$, and solve the
finite-field discrete logarithm $u = h^x$ for $0 \le x < \ord(g)$; the recovered
$x$ is unique.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

The abstraction shows exactly what each property costs in axioms: completeness
and aggregation are theorems of bare biadditivity; binding requires
nondegeneracy; antisymmetry requires the alternating axiom; the MOV reduction is
again bare biadditivity with $\ord(e(g,g))$ the sole quantitative input.
Aggregate BLS underpins finality gadgets of proof-of-stake blockchains, where
Theorem~\ref{thm:agg} explains why a single group element suffices to certify
thousands of votes. The MOV reduction is a design constraint: curve selection
must ensure a large embedding degree.

\section{Future work}

(1) On targets without 2-torsion, antisymmetry for all $p,q$ should be
equivalent to the alternating law, the gap being the 2-torsion subgroup.
(2) Promote Theorem~\ref{thm:movrec} to an \emph{iff} characterizing the
embedding degree $k$ as least $k$ with $\ord(g)\mid\ord(e(g,g))$ in
$\mu_{q^k-1}$. (3) Upgrade aggregate completeness to a soundness/binding
statement equivalent to left-nondegeneracy, via $e(\Delta, g)=1$ for the
difference of aggregates. (4) For cyclic $T$, reduce the full ECDLP to a single
modular solve.

\section{Conclusion}

From two equations --- biadditivity in each slot --- we obtain the complete BLS
protocol layer, its short aggregate variant, binding as point separation, the
alternating refinement defining the genuine Weil pairing, and the MOV reduction
as a faithful congruence governed by a single order. The analytic construction of
the Weil pairing is unnecessary for the protocol layer: the algebra of the
interface is the whole story.

